<!DOCTYPE html><html><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>Crop Pipeline Review 2026-06-29</title><style>
:root{--ink:#1c1c1a;--muted:#6b7280;--line:#e5e7eb;--bg:#fbfbf9;--accent:#2f6f4e;--terra:#aa5b1e;--terrabg:#fbf1e8;--greenbg:#eef6f0;--blue:#2563eb;--bluebg:#eef2ff}
*{box-sizing:border-box}
body{font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,"Segoe UI",Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;color:var(--ink);line-height:1.5;max-width:920px;margin:0 auto;padding:40px 28px 80px;background:var(--bg)}
h1{font-size:26px;margin:0 0 4px;letter-spacing:-.01em}
.sub{color:var(--muted);font-size:14px;margin-bottom:24px}
.glance{background:var(--greenbg);border:1px solid #cfe5d6;border-radius:10px;padding:14px 18px;font-size:14.5px;margin-bottom:30px}
.glance b{color:var(--accent)}
h2{font-size:19px;margin:38px 0 14px;padding-bottom:7px;border-bottom:2px solid var(--line)}
.dec{border:1px solid var(--line);border-left:4px solid var(--terra);background:#fff;border-radius:8px;padding:14px 18px;margin:12px 0}
.dec .q{font-weight:650;font-size:15.5px;margin-bottom:6px}
.dec .body{font-size:14px;color:#33332f;margin-bottom:8px}
.dec .rec{font-size:13.5px;background:var(--terrabg);border-radius:6px;padding:8px 11px}
.dec .rec b{color:var(--terra)}
.num{display:inline-block;width:22px;height:22px;line-height:22px;text-align:center;background:var(--terra);color:#fff;border-radius:50%;font-size:12px;font-weight:700;margin-right:8px;vertical-align:middle}
table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:13px;margin:10px 0 4px}
th{text-align:left;background:#f3f4f1;padding:7px 10px;border-bottom:2px solid var(--line);font-size:11.5px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.04em;color:#555}
td{padding:7px 10px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line);vertical-align:top}
tr:nth-child(even) td{background:#fcfcfb}
.pass{color:var(--accent);font-weight:650}
.tag{display:inline-block;font-size:11px;padding:1px 7px;border-radius:20px;background:#eef2ff;color:#3730a3}
.tag.new{background:var(--terrabg);color:var(--terra)}
code{background:#f3f4f1;padding:1px 5px;border-radius:4px;font-size:12.5px}
.fix td{font-size:12.5px}
.notable{font-size:13.5px}
.notable li{margin-bottom:5px}
.crop{font-weight:650}
.fyi{background:#f7f7f5;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:8px;padding:14px 18px;font-size:13px;color:#444}
.fyi h3{margin:0 0 8px;font-size:14px}
.foot{margin-top:40px;color:var(--muted);font-size:12px;border-top:1px solid var(--line);padding-top:14px}
</style></head><body>
<h1>Crop Pipeline Review</h1>
<div class="sub">2026-06-29 &middot; 32 crops authored (roster now 50) + the cool-season timing bug found &amp; fixed &middot; prepared for your review</div>

<div class="glance">
<b>At a glance:</b> the roster went from <b>18 to 50</b> in a day &mdash; <b>32 crops authored and gate-clean</b>
(13 promoted as drafts this afternoon, 19 more overnight). All pass the gate suite individually; the 18 certified
anchors still pass; the dataset-wide register + the cool-season timing audit are clean (<b>0 hits across all 50</b>).
The app session caught a real planting-timing bug in <b>broccoli</b> (certified/live) that one new crop inherited;
it's now <b>fixed and verified (16 issues &rarr; 0)</b>, and a guardrail stops it recurring. Nothing new is live or
committed &mdash; everything waits on your approvals. Your time is the <b>6 decisions</b> below; the rest is detail.
</div>

<h2>Decisions for you</h2>
<div class='dec'><div class='q'><span class='num'>1</span>Approve the timing-bug fix (broccoli + kohlrabi + carrot + radish)</div><div class='body'>The app session found a real bug: broccoli (certified, live) was planting cool-season crops in July across 4 warm-region cells where its peers (lettuce, carrot) heat_pause. kohlrabi cloned it. Fixed + independently verified: <b>16 audit hits &rarr; 0</b>, gate PASS, 18/18 certified hold, surgical diff (broccoli touches only those 4 cells, carrot only plant_out).</div><div class='rec'><b>My recommendation:</b> Approve as-is. One reading to confirm: for ca_south_coast, the UC table's literal “June–July” was read as the <i>seed-start</i> month and the fall transplant set to Sep–Oct (matching the peers), with heat_pause labeled Jun–Aug. Sound.</div></div>
<div class='dec'><div class='q'><span class='num'>2</span>Lock a rule: gate on the actual biology, not the template</div><div class='body'>The alliums/legumes sorted themselves out perfectly: garlic (vernalization), leek (no bulbing), and edamame (soybean maturity-group) all <i>dropped</i> onion's photoperiod gating — while shallot, which <i>does</i> bulb by day-length, correctly <i>kept</i> it. Forcing photoperiod where it doesn't belong is both biologically wrong and gate-failing.</div><div class='rec'><b>My recommendation:</b> Lock the principle: a crop carries <code>gating_factors</code> only if its own biology gates that way, regardless of what its template does.</div></div>
<div class='dec'><div class='q'><span class='num'>3</span>Garlic — confirm the overwinter model</div><div class='body'>Garlic is the hardest crop in the batch: a fall-plant → overwinter → summer-harvest lifecycle spanning the year boundary, hand-authored (the deriver can't wrap the year). Variety axis is vernalization (hardneck/softneck), not day-length. Gate-clean.</div><div class='rec'><b>My recommendation:</b> Confirm the vernalization model (vs. defining a separate garlic day-length model).</div></div>
<div class='dec'><div class='q'><span class='num'>4</span>Garlic — an app-render nuance to hand off</div><div class='body'>Garlic's winter <code>cold_pause</code> means “dormant in the ground — go mulch it,” not a warm crop's “nothing to do.” The app may render the two identically and mislead. This is a display note, not a data fix.</div><div class='rec'><b>My recommendation:</b> Hand to the app session as a follow-up (no dataset change).</div></div>
<div class='dec'><div class='q'><span class='num'>5</span>Carryover judgment calls (from the first 13)</div><div class='body'>Two real ones worth your nod: (1) <b>snow peas</b> — we <i>overrode</i> a Tier-1 source (UMD: “peas are heavy nitrogen users”) in favor of the consensus low-nitrogen guidance. (2) <b>turnip scab</b> — omitted because no clean turnip-specific source was readable (flagged rather than fabricated).</div><div class='rec'><b>My recommendation:</b> Confirm the snow-pea override; accept the turnip-scab omission or ask me to source it.</div></div>
<div class='dec'><div class='q'><span class='num'>6</span>Then: which of the 32 to certify</div><div class='body'>All 32 are gate-clean DRAFTS (author_fresh_pilot, not live). Certification = the launch-flip, gated on your read here + a per-crop source-truth spot-check. The systemic flags below (Hawaii / derived-harvest / etc.) are conventions you already blessed — listed as FYI, no action. (Also worth a moment: marigold + nasturtium handle their companion/pest claims with the right honesty — research-supported vs. traditional, kept separate.)</div><div class='rec'><b>My recommendation:</b> Tell me which to flip; I'll do the source-truth sample + the launch-flip on those. No rush — 32 is a lot to certify; we can do it in waves.</div></div>


<h2>The timing-bug fix (done &amp; verified)</h2>
<p style="font-size:14px">Broccoli was planting in July where its cool-season peers heat_pause, with a <code>plant_out</code> that
listed only winter. Realigned to the peer pattern (heat_pause through the hot months, plant the fall window) and
the calendar re-derived. Carrot + radish just had their missing <code>plant_out</code> authored (their timing was
already fine). <b>Verified:</b> audit 16&rarr;0 hits, every crop gate PASS, 18/18 certified hold, and the change is
surgical (broccoli touches only the 4 cells; carrot only <code>plant_out</code>).</p>
<table class="fix"><tr><th>Crops</th><th>Cells</th><th>Plant &mdash; before</th><th>Plant &mdash; after</th><th>Heat-pause</th><th>plant_out</th></tr>
<tr><td>broccoli + kohlrabi</td><td>ca_interior z8, z9</td><td>Jan, Feb, <b>Jul</b>, Dec</td><td>Jan, Feb, <b>Aug, Sep</b>, Dec</td><td>May, Jun &rarr; <b>May, Jun, Jul</b></td><td>“Dec 1 - Feb 28” &rarr; <b>“Aug 1 - Sep 30, Dec 1 - Feb 28”</b></td></tr>
<tr><td>broccoli + kohlrabi</td><td>ca_south_coast z9, z10</td><td>Jan, Feb, <b>Jun, Jul</b></td><td>Jan, Feb, <b>Sep, Oct</b></td><td>Aug &rarr; <b>Jun, Jul, Aug</b></td><td>“Jan 1 - Feb 28” &rarr; <b>“Sep 1 - Oct 31, Jan 1 - Feb 28”</b></td></tr>
</table>
<p style="font-size:13px;color:#444"><b>carrot + radish:</b> <code>plant_out</code> filled on all cells to match their existing plant months (e.g. carrot ca_south_coast &ldquo;Jan&ndash;Sep,&rdquo; radish se_gulf &ldquo;Aug&ndash;Sep, Dec&ndash;Feb&rdquo;). No calendar change.</p>

