</table>
<p style="font-size:12.5px;color:#6b7280">All sources are catalogued Tier-1 (university extension). “Flags” = honest open_findings, all non-blocking; the substantive ones are below, the rest are the conventions you already blessed.</p>

<h2>Notable per-crop flags (the substance)</h2>
<p style="font-size:13.5px;color:#444">Filtered to the judgment-worthy items &mdash; the systemic conventions (Hawaii-PDF, derived-harvest, germ-envelope, modeled regional windows, container figures, portal-level URLs) are folded into the FYI block below.</p>
<p style='margin:12px 0 3px'><span class='crop'>radish</span></p><ul class='notable'><li>heat_threshold_temp_f of 70 F (air) is a conservative synthesis: Clemson gives optimum 50-65 F and describes quality loss/bolting above that, but no single T1 &#x27;70 F&#x27; figure was located. Flagged for biology review.</li></ul><p style='margin:12px 0 3px'><span class='crop'>spinach</span></p><ul class='notable'><li>days_to_maturity [37, 50] (mid 45): Clemson states 37 to 45 days; UMD states 28 to 55 from seed. The [37, 50] range is a defensible mature-leaf span chosen from these, with baby-leaf noted as earlier in prose.</li><li>The northern-tier summer gap is marked season_over, not heat_pause, a deliberate modeling decision per the task and the certified onion precedent: spinach&#x27;s northern summer stop is primarily a long-day (photoperiod) bolt and end-of-cool-season effect, not the thermal exclusion the genuinely-hot regions carry. No heat_pause object is authored for any northern_tier or ca_north_coast cell.</li></ul><p style='margin:12px 0 3px'><span class='crop'>cabbage</span></p><ul class='notable'><li>The claim that cool weather and light frost improve cabbage flavor is stated mildly. Cold-hardiness is well sourced (Clemson: mature heads to 18 to 20 degrees F; USU: little damage to 25 degrees F), but UMN explicitly notes no flavor improvement, so frost-sweetening is framed as a softer, less-pronounced effect than in kale or brussels sprouts.</li><li>The northern-tier heat_pause is placed only in the WARMER zones (z6 July; z7 June-August) where midsummer genuinely excludes heading (Clemson Piedmont, Penn State fall-planting dates); cool z3 to z5 carry no heat_pause because cabbage crops through their moderate summers (Iowa State harvests spring cabbage into August and sets fall transplants mid-June to mid-July).</li></ul><p style='margin:12px 0 3px'><span class='crop'>pumpkin</span></p><ul class='notable'><li>spacing_inches is [36,72]. Full-size pumpkin hills run 4-8 ft apart with 6-12 ft rows (UGA: 8 ft hills, 72x48 in; UMN: 24-36 in in-row, 5-6 ft rows), but the numeric-sanity gate caps non-tree spacing at 72 in, so the 8-ft hill spacing is carried in prose (soil_prep, description, yield) rather than the numeric field.</li><li>The Phytophthora blight disease entry is authored from general cucurbit-disease knowledge and anchored to umn_ext (the pumpkin/winter-squash growing page) rather than a Phytophthora-specific page read this session; the other three diseases (powdery mildew, downy mildew, bacterial wilt) are directly source-verified.</li><li>yield_expectations (2-5 carving pumpkins per full-size vine; 10-20 ft vine spread) is a standard horticultural range cited to uga_c1206 + umn_ext but not pinned to an explicit per-plant yield figure on those pages.</li></ul><p style='margin:12px 0 3px'><span class='crop'>beet</span></p><ul class='notable'><li>A separate &#x27;beetroot&#x27; honest-shell record already exists in the dataset. This record was authored per the task&#x27;s explicit slug=&#x27;beet&#x27;. Flag for a dedup / canonical-slug ruling (keep &#x27;beet&#x27;, retire/merge &#x27;beetroot&#x27;, or vice versa) before launch.</li><li>Two T1 UC sources disagree on the south coast: the statewide UC ANR table lists &#x27;South Coast: Jan-Sept&#x27; (implying an autumn gap, no summer pause), while UC San Diego MG gives coastal &#x27;Sep-May&#x27; (a clean Jun-Aug summer heat_pause). Used San Diego, which gives the agronomically correct summer exclusion. Worth a ruling.</li></ul><p style='margin:12px 0 3px'><span class='crop'>cauliflower</span></p><ul class='notable'><li>days_to_maturity [50, 100] uses a directly sourced lower/common end (Snow Crown 50, Amazing/Early Snowball/White Contessa ~60 to 70, Denali 80 per USU/Iowa State/Clemson) and extends the upper bound to ~100 to cover long-season and overwintering/Romanesco types; the highest single sourced figure found was ~80 to 85 days, so the 80-to-100 tail is a modeled allowance for specialty long types.</li><li>Per-region planting windows are grounded in the same regional extension vegetable/cole-crop planting calendars the cabbage pilot cites; several list cauliflower together with broccoli and cabbage rather than as a standalone table, so cauliflower windows are taken as the cole-crop window for the region, tightened where cauliflower&#x27;s narrower heat and cold tolerance demands. Windows judged too warm for cauliflower&#x27;s curd were trimmed relative to cabbage (e.g. May harvest dropped on the Gulf coast and in the interior valleys; deep-cold December harvest trimmed in northern z4/z6).</li><li>Cauliflower is treated as LESS cold-hardy than cabbage and broccoli: plants take light frost but a developing or mature curd is damaged by a hard freeze below about 28°F, and young plants button from prolonged cold. USU states transplants can go out 1 to 2 weeks before last frost; the specific curd hard-freeze damage threshold (~28°F) is a modeled, widely held figure rather than a single quoted T1 number.</li></ul><p style='margin:12px 0 3px'><span class='crop'>turnip</span></p><ul class='notable'><li>days_to_maturity is set to [40, 60] (mid 50) to span the home-garden usable-root window: fast salad types (Hakurei, Tokyo) at roughly 35 to 40 days, the classic Purple Top White Globe at about 50 to 55, and greens harvestable sooner. Clemson and USU give 60 to 80 days for full tennis-ball-size roots, and UGA gives 50 to 60; the chosen range favors the harvest-at-usable-size norm and is noted in prose. The spread, not a single number, is the honest figure.</li><li>warm_arid (z8) and low_desert_az (z9) exact month charts live in TAMU/NMSU/UA materials not fully extracted; the windows are built from the readable TAMU fall-vegetable guide, UA Cooperative Extension low-desert calendar, and NMSU CR-457-B plus cool-season turnip physiology. se_gulf z9 deep-south dates lean on Clemson/LSU prose corroborated by MS/AL.</li><li>pH preferred_range is [6.0, 6.8] for growth (Clemson 6.0-6.5, UMN 6.0-7.5), but clubroot management calls for liming toward a neutral to slightly alkaline pH of about 7.2 to 7.5 (Purdue). The mild tension between ideal-growth pH and clubroot-suppressing pH is surfaced explicitly in the ph/soil/clubroot prose, with liming advised only on clubroot-prone beds.</li></ul><p style='margin:12px 0 3px'><span class='crop'>swiss-chard</span></p><ul class='notable'><li>days_to_maturity [50, 60] (mid 55) is synthesized: Clemson states 40 to 45 days direct-sown, UF/IFAS states 45 to 60 days to harvest, USU implies roughly 50 to 60 to full size; baby leaves come much sooner. The [50, 60] full-size span is a defensible midpoint of the sourced ranges, with the earlier baby-leaf and Clemson figures stated in prose.</li><li>The growth_stages &#x27;bolting&#x27; stage and the bolting block are reframed as a SECOND-YEAR / vernalization event, not a summer-heat or long-day event, per UMN (&#x27;will not flower until the plant has lived through a winter&#x27;) and the beet vernalization-bolting mechanism. First-year summer plants do not bolt; premature first-year bolting is attributed to too-early cold-soil sowing or a hard cold snap. bolting.risk = low.</li></ul><p style='margin:12px 0 3px'><span class='crop'>arugula</span></p><ul class='notable'><li>days_to_maturity [20, 40] (mid 30) is a synthesized span: Cornell and Wisconsin give baby leaf at 3 to 4 weeks and full leaf around 40 days, while Clemson states 45 to 60 days for the largest leaves. The [20, 40] range captures the dominant baby-to-standard-leaf window with the larger-leaf tail noted in prose.</li><li>spacing_inches [1, 3] reflects the dominant broadcast/baby-leaf practice; sources also give 4 to 6 inches (Cornell/Wisconsin) and up to 9 inches (Clemson) for large individual plants, stated in the thinning prose.</li><li>Per the task and arugula&#x27;s biology, the cool northern zones 3, 4, and 5 are modeled as RUNNING THROUGH summer (continuous succession, no heat_pause), while only the warmer northern zones 6 and 7 and the genuinely hot regions carry backed heat_pause objects (15 cells total). Arugula CAN bolt in northern midsummer; the region_notes state this, and the daily review may add a brief z5/z6 pause if warranted.</li></ul><p style='margin:12px 0 3px'><span class='crop'>snow-peas</span></p><ul class='notable'><li>archetype set to cool_season_annual, NOT green-beans-bush&#x27;s warm_season_fruiting. The task modeled snow peas structurally on the green-beans legume template but the dataset&#x27;s archetype encodes SEASON, not botanical family. Snow peas are cool-season, so cool_season_annual is the correct, gate-proven value.</li><li>The summer gap is a BACKED heat_pause object in the northern tier and all genuinely-hot regions (peas stop setting pods above about 80F, a true thermal exclusion), and season_over only in cool-maritime ca_north_coast (foggy summers stay cool, no heat exclusion). This mirrors the gate-clean spinach token choices and the task&#x27;s instruction to back the heat where hot.</li><li>UMD&#x27;s Garden Peas page states peas are &#x27;heavy users of nitrogen (peas fix little nitrogen),&#x27; which conflicts with the consensus (USU, UMN, Clemson, general legume biology): peas fix much of their own nitrogen and need little feeding. The record follows the consensus low-nitrogen legume framing and cites USU and Clemson (5-10-10 at 3 lb/100 sq ft pre-plant, avoid excess N) for fertilizer.</li></ul><p style='margin:12px 0 3px'><span class='crop'>butternut-squash</span></p><ul class='notable'><li>spacing_inches is [24,72]. Butternut is spaced 24-36 in in the row with 5-6 ft (60-72 in) between rows per UMN, and full-size vines run 8-12 ft, but the numeric-sanity gate caps non-tree spacing at 72 in, so the row/sprawl figures are carried in prose (soil_prep, description, yield) rather than the numeric field.</li></ul><p style='margin:12px 0 3px'><span class='crop'>garlic</span></p><ul class='notable'><li>gating_factors set to [] rather than onion&#x27;s [photoperiod]. Garlic IS photoperiod-responsive for bulbing (long days induce bulbing), but its varieties are classified hardneck vs softneck by VERNALIZATION (cold need), not by short/intermediate/long-day cultivar class, so there is no honest day_length_type to assign. The A9 window-fit gate (B4) also encodes onion&#x27;s assumption that long_day = spring-planted; garlic is a fall-planted, long-day-bulbing crop, which that rule structurally rejects. Choosing [photoperiod] would force either biologically false day_length_type labels or an A9 failure. Flagged for Trevor: confirm [] (vernalization-driven, like the strawberry photoperiod-INVERSE precedent), or rule a garlic-specific gating model.</li><li>In cold regions the deep-winter overwintering months are modeled as cold_pause (the frost-bracketed dormant token). For garlic this means crop-in-ground-but-dormant, which differs from a warm-season annual&#x27;s cold_pause (no crop planted). The app may render both identically, so a grower could read cold_pause as &#x27;nothing to do&#x27; when garlic is actually overwintering in the bed and needs mulch. Flagged as a possible token-semantics gap for the overwinter lifecycle; no dedicated dormant-resident token exists today.</li><li>lifecycle set to &#x27;perennial&#x27; (garlic is botanically a vegetatively propagated perennial, mirroring onion&#x27;s biological &#x27;biennial&#x27;) while perennial:false marks it as grown as a single-cycle crop (not a standing perennial in the planner). Flagged in case the project prefers lifecycle &#x27;annual&#x27; to reflect cultivation; chose the biology token to mirror onion&#x27;s convention.</li><li>fl_peninsula is modeled from UF/IFAS qualitative guidance (warm winters require pre-chilling cloves about two months; softneck/Creole or elephant garlic only; hardnecks fail). The specific month windows (plant Nov-Dec, harvest Mar-May) are inferred from that guidance plus the regional climate, not a dated FL garlic planting chart. Modeled; the daily review should confirm against a FL-specific source if one is catalogued.</li></ul><p style='margin:12px 0 3px'><span class='crop'>leek</span></p><ul class='notable'><li>days_to_maturity [90, 150] spans early types (King Richard ~90 to 100) to slow overwintering types (~150). Sourced figures cluster at 100 to 120 (UMN/USU), 70 to 120 (UMD), and 100 to 130 (UF/IFAS); the upper tail to 150 is a modeled allowance for overwintering varieties.</li><li>The CA regions cite uc_mg (UC Master Gardener time-of-planting) for timing, but that table lists ONIONS, not leeks. It is used as the nearest allium analog and the windows are adjusted for leek&#x27;s longer season and lack of photoperiod bulbing.</li></ul><p style='margin:12px 0 3px'><span class='crop'>parsnip</span></p><ul class='notable'><li>days_to_maturity set to [100,130] (mid 120). Texas A&amp;M gives 100 to 120 days; Clemson gives a longer 120 to 180 for late South Carolina crops. The record uses the 100 to 120 mainstream figure and notes the longer late-crop range in prose. Worth a reviewer confirm of the headline range.</li><li>Carrot rust fly and leafminer symptom prose is standard extension knowledge cited to USU&#x27;s root-crop pest list and RHS, which name the pests for the carrot/parsnip group; the dedicated UC IPM / UMN symptom pages were not separately readable (404/redirect). The canker (UMass) and aster yellows (Wisconsin Horticulture) entries are directly sourced.</li></ul><p style='margin:12px 0 3px'><span class='crop'>cilantro-coriander</span></p><ul class='notable'><li>All 10 regions&#x27; per-zone calendars are MODELED from cilantro&#x27;s cool-season frost/heat envelope and the regional extension planting-calendar sources, reusing the gate-clean arugula/spinach cool-season calendar SHAPE (spring + fall plant windows, summer heat_pause in warm regions, cold_pause in winter, overwinter in mild zones). Few cilantro-per-region charts exist; the frost-anchored cool-season shape is shared climate truth across cool-season leafy crops, and the cilantro-specific refit is its dual leaf/seed use and rapid heat bolt. resolution_method on each cell is &#x27;cc_author_pilot_modeled&#x27;. Flagged for the daily biology-fidelity review.</li><li>ph.preferred_range [6.2, 6.8] follows the commonly cited cilantro optimum (slightly acidic to neutral); Clemson states a working range of 6 to 7, used as the tolerated band [6.0, 7.5]. Cilantro is not pH-sensitive.</li></ul><p style='margin:12px 0 3px'><span class='crop'>edamame</span></p><ul class='notable'><li>archetype set to warm_season_fruiting (matching the green-beans-bush template the task specified), with calendar_basis frost_anchored. Edamame is a warm-season legume planted after frost into warm soil, so the warm-season calendar (winter cold_pause, spring/summer plant, summer/fall harvest) is correct.</li><li>Several pest/disease anchors are soybean-production or general-bean pages at the publisher level rather than edamame-home-garden-specific: bean leaf beetle (mu_ext G7150), stink bug / soybean aphid / SCN (umn_ext soybean pest management, iastate_ext soybean diseases index). The pathology is shared (edamame IS soybean), and white mold / bacterial blight / spider mite / aphid overlap the green-beans legume set.</li></ul><p style='margin:12px 0 3px'><span class='crop'>eggplant</span></p><ul class='notable'><li>Eggplant RUNS THROUGH summer heat (no heat_pause) in every region except the three desert-extreme cells (ca_desert z9/z10, low_desert_az z9), where a backed heat_pause (months [7,8]) is modeled: even this heat-lover&#x27;s pollen loses viability at 110F+ days / 80F+ nights, so the crop splits into a spring and a fall planting. The pause months and the spring/fall window split are modeled from the regional warm-season pattern, not a live desert eggplant calendar; the thermal basis (extreme-heat pollen sterility) is sound horticulture.</li><li>ph.preferred_range is [5.5, 6.8], honoring the task steer and UMN&#x27;s stated 5.5-7.5 best range; Clemson cites a narrower 6.0-6.5 sweet spot (carried in the prose as the sweet spot). The first prose range matches the structured preferred_range for A34 consistency.</li></ul><p style='margin:12px 0 3px'><span class='crop'>tomatillo</span></p><ul class='notable'><li>Heat-tolerance refit: tomatillo is modeled to RUN THROUGH summer in the SE/Gulf and warm-arid Southwest (where tomato heat_pauses), with a backed heat_pause retained only in the extreme low desert (ca_desert, low_desert_az; Jun-Aug) and the FL peninsula deep summer (Jul-Aug). SDSU notes flowers drop &gt;90F, so set is reduced (not zero) in milder humid summers; the run-through is a deliberate, honest heat-tolerance call to confirm in review.</li><li>Companion pairings (basil, marigold, nasturtium; avoid fennel and Solanaceae) are inherited from tomato tradition with honest provenance labels (traditional/likely/mechanistic); the only extension-backed pairings are the self-pollination second-plant and the Solanaceae rotation rule. No tomatillo-specific companion trials located.</li><li>pH preferred range 6.0-7.0 (tolerated 5.5-7.3) is sourced to general extension/horticultural guidance for tomatillo; NCSU/USU pages confirm full-sun, well-drained culture but do not all state an explicit pH band on the page read. Value is consistent across sources and with the Solanaceae norm.</li></ul><p style='margin:12px 0 3px'><span class='crop'>okra</span></p><ul class='notable'><li>days_to_maturity set to [50,65] to first pods (days_to_maturity_mid 57). Clemson and UGA state 60-70; Alabama 45-55; UF 50-70; fast hybrids (Cajun Delight ~52, Jambalaya ~50) anchor the low end. The [50,65] band spans fast-to-standard varieties to first pods, after which okra bears continuously.</li></ul><p style='margin:12px 0 3px'><span class='crop'>parsley</span></p><ul class='notable'><li>ph.preferred_range [6.0, 7.0] is the UMN-stated best range for parsley; tolerated [5.5, 7.5] is a modeled widening. Parsley is not pH-sensitive.</li><li>Pests (parsleyworm/black swallowtail, aphids, carrot rust fly and carrot weevil) and diseases (Septoria leaf spot, crown/root rot, damping-off) are drawn from the parsley/herb pages (Clemson and Wisconsin name parsley as a black swallowtail host with seldom-damaging numbers and aphids as the most common pest; Wisconsin names Septoria as the most common disease). The carrot rust fly/weevil and the crown-rot and damping-off details are the standard Apiaceae set applied to parsley and anchored to the institution herb/vegetable pages; flagged for the source-truth sample.</li></ul><p style='margin:12px 0 3px'><span class='crop'>dill</span></p><ul class='notable'><li>All 10 regions&#x27; per-zone calendars are MODELED from dill&#x27;s cool-season frost/heat envelope and the regional extension planting-calendar sources, reusing the gate-clean cilantro/arugula/spinach cool-season calendar SHAPE (spring + fall plant windows, summer heat_pause in warm regions, cold_pause in winter). Few dill-per-region charts exist; the frost-anchored cool-season shape is shared climate truth across cool-season bolt-prone herbs, and the dill-specific refit is its dual leaf/seed use and summer bolt. resolution_method on each cell is &#x27;cc_author_pilot_modeled&#x27;. Flagged for the daily biology-fidelity review.</li><li>ph.preferred_range [5.5, 6.5] is Utah State University&#x27;s stated ideal range for dill; the tolerated band [5.5, 7.0] reflects dill&#x27;s forgiving, not-pH-sensitive nature (harvest-to-table gives 5.5 to 6.7).</li><li>The bolt threshold stated as &#x27;roughly 75 to 80°F and lengthening days&#x27; is MODELED. The readable T1 dill pages state that dill bolts in &#x27;hot weather&#x27; and long days and that heat reduces seed production (USU notes +95°F decreases seed; Wisconsin: hot or dry triggers the flower stalk), but none table a precise bolt temperature. The ~75 to 80°F figure follows the cool-season bolt convention (cilantro ~75°F) and is surfaced consistently in the heat_pause basis, bolting, and weather-trigger prose.</li><li>Powdery mildew is directly sourced (USU and Wisconsin both name it on dill). The leaf-spot/blight and damping-off entries are anchored to the same readable T1 dill pages, but the specific pathogen IDs (Cercosporidium punctum / Alternaria for leaf blight; Pythium/Rhizoctonia/Fusarium for damping-off) are MODELED from general Apiaceae disease knowledge: the Pacific Northwest Pest Management Handbooks document dill Alternaria leaf spot and damping-off but returned HTTP 403 and were not WebFetch-readable, so per the sourcing rule they are flagged rather than extracted.</li></ul><p style='margin:12px 0 3px'><span class='crop'>marigold</span></p><ul class='notable'><li>FRAMING (not a sourcing gap): root-knot nematode SUPPRESSION by French marigold (Tagetes patula) is framed as research-supported ONLY for a dense, weed-free solid COVER/preceding stand grown ~2 months then incorporated (UF/IFAS ENY-056, Clemson root-knot nematode factsheet, Iowa State, LSU AgCenter); UF/IFAS explicitly states casual interplanting among vegetables has NOT been shown to reduce nematodes, and African types are less effective than French. The broader insect/rabbit/deer scent-repellent companion reputation is framed as TRADITIONAL belief throughout, not extension-verified. This honesty boundary is load-bearing for the companion and tip prose.</li></ul><p style='margin:12px 0 3px'><span class='crop'>shallot</span></p><ul class='notable'><li>Spacing 6 to 8 inches: USU gives 3 to 6 inches for sets and UF/IFAS about 6 inches; the 8-inch upper end is modeled to give each multiplying clump room (wider spacing yields fuller clumps per USU&#x27;s density note).</li></ul><p style='margin:12px 0 3px'><span class='crop'>watermelon</span></p><ul class='notable'><li>spacing_inches is [36,72]. Full-size watermelon hills run 4-8 ft apart with 6-8 ft rows and about 24 sq ft per plant (Clemson 5-6 ft in-row, 6-8 ft rows; UGA 8 ft hills; USU mounds 4 ft / rows 4-6 ft), but the numeric-sanity gate caps non-tree spacing at 72 in, so the true hill and row spacing is carried in prose (soil_prep, description, yield) rather than the numeric field.</li><li>Unlike the gate-clean pumpkin cousin (which carries a Jul-Aug heat_pause in the CA desert), watermelon is modeled with NO heat_pause in any cell. Watermelon is heat-loving and produces through southern and desert summer heat, so the hot regions (ca_desert, low_desert_az, warm_arid, se_gulf, fl_peninsula) run a continuous summer crop or a spring-plus-late-summer split for crop-turnover, not a heat exclusion. Florida&#x27;s deep-summer gap is disease/rain-driven (wet season), not heat, and is represented as a growing bridge rather than a pause token.</li><li>The seedless (triploid) pollinizer requirement is authored into pollinator_notes, varieties, start_method, companions, and the flowering tips/notifications, with the ratio (one seeded plant per two to three seedless, or a seeded row within about 10 ft) cited to clemson_hgic + umn_ext + uga_ext. The exact ratio varies by source and is given as a representative range.</li></ul><p style='margin:12px 0 3px'><span class='crop'>cucumber</span></p><ul class='notable'><li>ph.tolerated_range [5.5,7.0] is the general cucurbit tolerance band; the preferred [6.0,6.5] is T1 (UMN, Clemson). The tolerated endpoints are not each sole-anchored to a cucumber-specific statement.</li></ul><p style='margin:12px 0 3px'><span class='crop'>sunflower</span></p><ul class='notable'><li>ALLELOPATHY FRAMING (load-bearing honesty boundary, not a sourcing gap): Helianthus annuus is documented as an allelopathic species at the species level (NC State plant toolbox tags H. annuus #allelopathic; Penn State &#x27;Allelopathy in the Home Garden&#x27; lists sunflowers among allelopathic plants; UF/IFAS recognizes the phenomenon), and seed hulls are widely documented as the classic inhibitory residue (the bare patch under bird feeders). The SPECIFIC sensitive-neighbor list (potatoes, pole/runner beans) is well-reported companion-planting tradition rather than a single extension trial, and reports conflict (some hold beans tolerate it; cucurbits/corn/squash tolerate it comparatively well). The companion &#x27;bad&#x27; entries are therefore labeled likely/medium (potatoes, hull residue) and traditional/low (pole beans), with the species-level allelopathy verified_against_sources:true and the crop-specific calls hedged. The extension sources name the phenomenon but do not enumerate the garden-crop sensitivity list, so the specific pairings are the reviewer&#x27;s confirm item.</li><li>DAYS_TO_MATURITY [60,100] (mid 80) and start_method represent type-spanning judgment calls. Sunflower bloom timing ranges widely by type: fast single-stem cut-flower types bloom in ~55 to 65 days while giants take 100 to 120+ days (MU Extension states 70 to 120 days to bloom); [60,100] mid 80 is a representative central range consistent with the carried zinnia harvest offsets, with the full spread described in prose. start_method.start is set to &#x27;both&#x27; (not &#x27;direct&#x27;) because UMN, Illinois, Clemson, and MU all document indoor starting as a real option, but the DIRECT-SOW preference and taproot transplant-sensitivity are the dominant framing throughout (weeks_before shortened to 3, biodegradable-pot caveat) per the task&#x27;s direct-sow emphasis. category retained as the shell&#x27;s &#x27;Edible &amp; Harvest&#x27; (not zinnia&#x27;s &#x27;Companion &amp; Pollinator&#x27;) to honor sunflower&#x27;s genuine seed-harvest use, which also drives the real (non-&#x27;not applicable&#x27;) storage and harvest fields.</li></ul><p style='margin:12px 0 3px'><span class='crop'>nasturtium</span></p><ul class='notable'><li>COMPANION FRAMING (not a sourcing gap): the APHID TRAP CROP function is framed as research/extension-supported (UMN reports nasturtium reduces pest pressure planted near brassicas, beans, squash, and pumpkin because aphids prefer it; UC ANR documents the trap-crop/sacrificial use), with the load-bearing qualifier that efficacy is placement-dependent (a stand set a few feet off, monitored, and destroyed once infested). Nasturtium also drawing flea beetles is framed as part of the same trap dynamic. The broader claim that nasturtium scent DETERS squash bugs and cucumber beetles from cucurbits is framed throughout as TRADITIONAL companion lore, not extension-verified. Beneficial-insect/pollinator recruitment is labeled likely. This honesty boundary (supported aphid-trap vs traditional deterrence) is load-bearing for the companion, tip, and pest prose.</li></ul>

<h2>Systemic conventions &mdash; FYI, no action</h2>
<div class="fyi">
<h3>You've already blessed these; they recur across crops and are flagged honestly:</h3>
&bull; <b>Hawaii windows</b> modeled on CTAHR cool-season/elevation guidance (its bulletin is an unreadable 1943 scanned PDF).<br>
&bull; <b>Harvest months derived</b> from the sourced planting window + days-to-maturity (extension charts publish planting, not harvest).<br>
&bull; <b>Germination-temp envelopes</b> from the crop family where the species page gives germination days, not min/max temps.<br>
&bull; <b>Desert / warm-arid windows</b> modeled on the regional cole-crop rule where the only source is a scanned PDF.<br>
&bull; <b>Marginal subtropical fits</b> (FL z11, lowland Hawaii) labeled honestly.<br>
&bull; <b>Container figures, portal-level source URLs, CC-derived successions</b> &mdash; routine modeling, all flagged.
